== 1. grape\_pipeline (nbody)


=== 1.1 Hardware description

#strong[What it is.] The name follows #emph[GRAPE] (\"GRAvity PipE\"), the University of Tokyo family of special-purpose N-body machines (GRAPE-1 1990 … GRAPE-6 2003): hard-wire the pairwise-force formula as a pipeline, leave the rest to a host (#raw("research/hw-algorithms-nbody.md §1")). #raw("grape_pipeline") keeps that idea and departs from it twice, both forced by the benchmark (#raw("hw/docs/adr/0002-grape-fp64-datapath-and-tolerance-oracle.md")): (1) #strong[full IEEE-754 FP64 in the benchmark's own operation order] — GRAPE's reduced-precision pairs (LNS/FP32-class, 1e-3…1e-6 force error) visibly diverge from the Python energy trace; (2) #strong[the whole #raw("advance()") runs on-device] — body state stays on-chip for all 20,000 steps after one doorbell, where GRAPE returned forces to a host integrator.


FP64 pairwise-gravity accelerator executing a full #raw("advance(dt, n)") on-device. SystemVerilog: #raw("rtl/grape_pipeline.sv") (top), #raw("rtl/grape_force_pipe.sv") (scheduled datapath), #raw("rtl/grape_accum.sv") (accumulate/integrate, parallel-prefix picker), #raw("rtl/grape_step_fsm.sv") (control), #raw("rtl/fp64_*.sv") (add/mul/sqrt/rcp units). Single clock domain, synchronous #raw("rst_n").


=== 1.2 Inputs / outputs, widths, frequency

- #strong[I/O] (#raw("docs/mas.md §2")): 32-bit AXI4-Lite slave exposing FP64 (binary64) body state, #raw("DT") (FP64), #raw("NSTEPS") (32-bit), pair configuration, control/status, counters, IRQ.
- #strong[Operating frequency:] design target #strong[50 MHz] (#raw("docs/mas.md §2") clk row, #raw("docs/prd.md") K-table). Achievable #strong[≈ 19.46 MHz] — #emph[post-CTS STA] on the parallel-prefix netlist (#raw("docs/ppa.md") Addendum 2026-09-14; evidence #raw("synth/evidence/ws.max.rpt"): +98.6212 ns setup slack \@ 150 ns period → 51.38 ns).

=== 1.3 Architecture

#raw("docs/uarch.md"): three FP64 add/sub, three multipliers, one sqrt (radix-4 SRT), one reciprocal (2¹⁰×20b ROM seed + Newton refinement in Q1.57), sharing a scheduled #strong[290-operation step]. An ordered accumulation sequencer resolves body-component dependencies (pairs sharing a body serialize their velocity updates) before positions commit; steps stay serial. There is no FMA — multiply and accumulate round separately (matches the benchmark's #raw("pow")-free arithmetic contract, #raw("docs/testplan.md §4")).


#strong[Generality.] The RTL is generated for #raw("N_BODIES = 5") (#raw("rtl/grape_regs.sv"): body window #raw("N_BODIES × 7") FP64 words in flops; pair list ≤ 10; the 290-op step's issue schedule is a 200-event ROM emitted by #raw("docs/gen_reservation.py") for that pair list (#raw("rtl/grape_sched_rom.svh")), the remaining accumulate and integrate operations being ordered dynamically). Larger N keeps the datapath, the register interface and the ordered accumulation but moves body state to SRAM, regenerates the schedule and — §1.5's model — wants more units. The brief's preference for hardware that is \"not too workload-specific\" is met at the level of the operation (pairwise force + ordered accumulate, the GRAPE recipe), not of this instance.


=== 1.4 HW/SW interface

- MMIO only, no DMA (#raw("docs/mas.md §5"), ADR-0001). ≈ 150 AXI-Lite transactions per invocation (70 body + 10 pair + 4 config in; 60 state + 3 counter out), \*\*once per 20,000-step invocation\*\* — \< 0.03 % of compute (#raw("docs/integration.md §1")).
- Driver model #raw("driver/grape_pipeline_driver.py") verified register-for-register vs #raw("docs/mas.md §4") (#raw("driver/check_regmap.py") → #strong[0 differences]), against a full-invocation cycle model (#raw("driver/test_driver.py") → 4/4 pass; 2,480,000 cycles = 124 × 20,000), and #strong[co-simulated against the RTL] through the pyuvm AXI-Lite agent (#raw("tb/test_driver_model.py"), #raw("make -C hw/grape_pipeline sim MODULE=test_driver_model")): a full #raw("advance(0.01, 2)") over the register protocol on the benchmark's five bodies and ten pairs reads back all 35 state components bit-identical to the golden model, CYCLES = 250 (125/step for a 2-step run; 124 is the 20,000-step asymptote), DONE cleared by W1C.

=== 1.5 Acceleration justification + estimate

- #strong[Baseline] #raw("T") = #strong[231.20 ms] (pyperf mean; median 229 ms), #raw("results/timing_distributions.txt:2") (231.204), rounded in #raw("results/baseline_nbody_stats.txt").
- #strong[Fraction] #raw("f") = 0.95 (#raw("advance") doubly-nested loop, #raw("docs/integration.md §3")).
- #strong[HW cost] 2,480,000 cycles/invocation (K1 = #strong[124] cyc/step, #raw("docs/ppa.md"), measured bit-exact on the full 20,000-step benchmark).
- Amdahl #raw("S = T / ((1−f)·T + t_hw)"):
  - #strong[50 MHz] (target): t\_hw = 49.6 ms → #strong[3.78×] at f = 0.95 (4.45× at f = 0.99); the speedup KPI (≥ 4×) is not met at the stated #raw("f").
  - #strong[19.46 MHz] (achievable): t\_hw = 127.4 ms → #strong[≈ 1.66×] (compute-only upper bound 1.81× vs the original / 1.12× vs the 143.13 ms optimized-Python tier). Does #strong[not] beat the 9.53 ms native-software tier. (#raw("docs/integration.md §3").)
- #strong[Bottom line vs the software tiers] (hardware rows are #emph[projections]: RTL cycles ÷ STA clock + 5 % Python residual; software rows are VM measurements, #raw("report_nbody") §4):

#table(columns: (auto, auto, 1fr), align: (left, right, right),
  table.header([#strong[Tier]], [#strong[Time / run]], [#strong[vs original]]),
  [Original Python], [231.20 ms], [1.00×],
  [Optimized Python], [143.13 ms], [1.62×],
  [Native Rust], [9.53 ms], [24.26×],
  [#strong[Hardware \@ 19.46 MHz]], [#strong[≈ 139 ms]], [#strong[≈ 1.66×]],
  [Hardware \@ 50 MHz (target)], [≈ 61 ms], [≈ 3.8×],
)


The accelerator is level with optimized Python (1.03×) and ≈ 15× slower than native Rust (≈ 6× at target). Per step: 124 cycles = 6.4 µs at 19.46 MHz vs 0.48 µs natively; matching Rust needs ≈ 260 MHz, and the 11.6 ms Python residual alone already exceeds Rust's 9.53 ms. The benchmark's cost was interpreter overhead, so removing the interpreter captures nearly all of the gain; at N = 5 with ordered pair dependencies there is little parallelism to exploit.

- #strong[Larger N (model projection, not RTL):] #raw("docs/schedule_model_n.py") runs the same schedule model (123 modeled vs 124 measured cycles/step at N = 5) for N bodies. N = 5 is latency-bound at 12.3 cycles/pair (one pair's chain is ≈ 80 cycles and ten pairs cannot fill it); at N = 100 the as-built units reach the unit-bound 4.36 cycles/pair = 0.224 µs/pair at 19.46 MHz — ≈ 4.4× faster than rolled Python (0.98 µs/pair) but 5.0× slower than native (0.045 µs/pair, #raw("results/bigN_sweep.txt")). 12 add + 12 mul → 1.10 cycles/pair (1.25× slower than native); 24 add + 24 mul + 2 sqrt + 2 rcp → 0.56 (1.6× faster). Upper bounds: they assume the clock survives wider issue selection (our 1-wide vs 3-wide data says it degrades), SRAM body state, and direct summation remaining the software competitor (Barnes-Hut wins above N ≈ 300).
- #strong[Honest conclusion:] compute-bound; the end-to-end win is gated on timing closure, not the interface. The limiter is the integrate-multiplier operand path, whose pipelining is the documented next step.

