== 2. huffman\_engine (pyflate)


=== 2.1 Hardware description

bzip2/DEFLATE canonical-Huffman decoder. SystemVerilog: #raw("rtl/huffman_engine.sv") (top) plus, in the same #raw("rtl/") folder, #raw("huff_aligner") (64-bit window + barrel shifter + FIFO), #raw("huff_builder") (canonical table build), #raw("huff_decoder") (20-wide comparator cascade + priority encode), #raw("huff_tables") (symtab + 6 table register sets), #raw("huff_regs") (length window + counters), #raw("huff_selector") (50-symbol table switch), #raw("huff_ctrl"), #raw("huff_deflate"), #raw("huff_out"). Single clock, #raw("rst_n").


=== 2.2 Inputs / outputs, widths, frequency

- #strong[I/O:] 32-bit compressed stream + 8-bit selector stream in; 32-bit beats out carrying a 9-bit symbol + type fields (#raw("docs/mas.md §2")). 32-bit AXI4-Lite window holds #raw("START_BIT"), lengths, counters for six tables and bzip2's 20-bit max code length.
- #strong[Operating frequency:] target #strong[50 MHz] (clock KPI, #raw("docs/prd.md") K-table). Achievable #strong[≈ 39.9 MHz] — #emph[post-CTS STA] at a 40 ns constraint: worst setup slack +14.941 ns → 25.06 ns achievable; no setup or hold violations (evidence preserved in #raw("hw/huffman_engine/synth/evidence/"); a tighter 27 ns run also meets timing and gives 39.5 MHz, a looser 120 ns run 31.7 MHz). The pre-placement estimate of the first 20 ns run is superseded (#raw("docs/ppa.md §3")).

=== 2.3 Architecture

#raw("docs/uarch.md"): a bit aligner feeds a canonical-code comparator/lookup datapath; a selector controller changes the active table every 50 symbols with a #strong[0-cycle switch] (six table register sets pre-loaded). Decodes 1 symbol/cycle.


=== 2.4 HW/SW interface

- Software parses each bzip2 block header and calls #raw("HuffmanDriver.decode_block()") over a 4 KB AXI4-Lite window; compressed bytes + selectors streamed by DMA (#raw("docs/mas.md §5/§6")).
- Driver model #raw("driver/huffman_engine_driver.py"), verified vs register map (0 diffs) and the signed-off cycle model (#raw("docs/integration.md §1")).

=== 2.5 Acceleration justification + estimate

- #strong[Baseline] #raw("T") = #strong[1,123.49 ms] (≈ 1.12 s mean; median 1.12 s), #raw("results/timing_distributions.txt:6") (1123.488), rounded in #raw("results/baseline_pyflate_stats.txt:18").
- #strong[Fraction] #raw("f") ≈ #strong[0.40] on the VM py-spy profile that #raw("report_pyflate") §2 uses (#raw("find_next_symbol") 12.63 % + #raw("readbits") 9.95 % + #raw("snoopbits") 9.41 % + #raw("_mask") 5.91 % + #raw("_more") 2.42 % of 372 samples, #raw("results/profile_functions.txt")). The PRD sized the module from a local cProfile run (#raw("docs/prd.md §1"): f = 0.496, 12.0 % decode + 37.6 % bit reader); cProfile's per-call overhead inflates the share of the many tiny bit-reader calls, so the sampled VM profile is the fraction used here and in the reports, and #raw("mtf_cam") (§3.5) is sized on the same profile.
- #strong[HW cost] 149,276 cycles for 148,271 symbols → #strong[K1 = 1.0068] cyc/sym, trace-exact vs golden over every beat (#raw("docs/ppa.md"), #raw("docs/uarch.md §7")).
- Amdahl ceiling at f = 0.40: #strong[1/(1 − 0.40) = 1.67×] standalone, for any clock at which the decode takes milliseconds (t\_hw = 3.74 ms at 39.9 MHz against a 674 ms residual). The earlier cProfile fraction gave 1.98×; the difference is the profiler, not the hardware.
- #strong[This standalone figure is superseded] by the chain analysis in §3.8: the two pyflate modules ship together, the replaced stage is measured at the Rust boundary (3.30 ms), and the end-to-end projection is \*\*≈ 6.6× vs the original — a tie with the delivered Python + Rust path\*\*, because once the symbol stage leaves the interpreter it is ≈ 2 % of what remains.

