  [Size], [151,058 cells / 1.634 mm²], [18,814 cells / 0.187 mm² (8.7× smaller)],
  [Cycles / symbol], [1.0068], [1.0686],
  [Post-CTS clock], [39.9 MHz], [37.5 MHz],
  [Limits the chain by], [—], [both: the slower clock #emph[and] the higher cycles/symbol],
)


#strong[Clocking of the chain.] Both modules are single-clock synchronous (#raw("docs/mas.md"): \"one clock domain, no CDC\"); the chain is specified on #strong[one shared clock, no clock-domain crossing], linked by an AXI4-Stream valid/ready handshake (beat format ADR-0006). Shared clock ⇒ the chain runs at the slower module's frequency, #strong[37.5 MHz (mtf-limited)]; huffman's 39.9 MHz estimate leaves it a little headroom. The modules differ in #emph[cycles per symbol] (1.0068 vs 1.0686), handled by #raw("tready") back-pressure: the chain advances at mtf's rate (159,303 cycles/block, measured). #strong[Verification status:] each side is verified standalone against the same beat contract and golden symbol stream (huffman trace-exact 148,271 beats; mtf byte-exact 336,184 B; back-pressure injected in both testbenches; handshakes formally checked — huffman #raw("skid_bmc/cover"), mtf #raw("hs/hscover")). #strong[The chain is also simulated together:] #raw("hw/pyflate_accel/") (wrapper #raw("hw/pyflate_accel/rtl/pyflate_accel.sv") + cocotb #raw("hw/pyflate_accel/tb/test_chain.py"); #raw("make -C hw/pyflate_accel sim") → 2/2 PASS) runs the real benchmark block through both modules: #strong[byte-exact over 336,184 bytes, with and without 50 % random output back-pressure; 159,303 cycles] (189,448 under back-pressure) — 0.54 % above the standalone projection (decoder table-build start-up). 148,271 link beats, 0 malformed; decoder stalled by mtf on 10,013 cycles, mtf starved on 873 — the chain runs at mtf's rate.


#strong[Same boundary as the Rust kernel] (148,271 symbols → 336,184 bytes). Hardware rows are #strong[measured chain RTL cycles] ÷ post-CTS clock (shared 37.5 MHz), not including DMA/interface time — the cycle count is measured, the clock is a static-timing estimate; the Rust figure is a phase-isolated VM measurement (#raw("report_appendix") A3, #raw("results/pyflate_phase_cpi.txt")); the Python figure is derived across separate experiments.


